## Images & Illustration Assets

Do not source stock imagery, and do not use Unsplash or any other photography.

### Hand-author the illustrations as inline SVG

Do NOT generate images with an AI image tool, and do NOT download stock assets.
Write the SVG by hand, as React components.

This is deliberate. These are sparse line drawings — a layered cake is a handful
of arcs and strokes — and hand-authoring gives three things the alternatives
cannot:

* The stroke inherits `currentColor`, so illustrations pick up the theme palette
  automatically and stay consistent with the type color
* Perfectly uniform stroke weight across the whole set, because you control it
* No files to load, no 404s, no licensing questions

### Drawing rules — apply to every illustration

* One consistent `stroke-width` across the entire set (start at 1.5 on a 24×24
  viewBox and scale proportionally for larger art). This single value is what
  makes the set read as one system — never vary it between illustrations.
* `fill="none"`, `stroke="currentColor"`
* `stroke-linecap="round"` and `stroke-linejoin="round"` — the rounded caps are
  what make it feel handcrafted rather than technical
* Deliberately sparse: suggest the form with a few clean strokes. No shading, no
  hatching, no interior detail, no text
* Generous negative space inside the viewBox — do not fill the frame
* Consistent viewBox proportions within each group (all category icons the same
  size, all product illustrations the same size)

### Structure

* One component per illustration in `src/components/illustrations/`, e.g.
  `LayeredCake.tsx`, `Tart.tsx`, `GiftBox.tsx`
* Each accepts `className` so size and color are controlled by the consumer
* Export them through a single index, and map illustration keys in
  `src/data/content.ts` to components so any one can be swapped in one line
* No `/public/images/` directory and no `next/image` for these — they are inline
  SVG, not image files

### What to draw

* **Hero (1)** — one elegant layered cake or tart. The most refined piece of the
  set; spend the most time here.
* **Categories (7)** — birthday cake, café cake slice, dry pastry/cookie,
  dessert cup, a leaf/sprig for the healthy line, a jar for the spreads, a gift
  box
* **Products (~6)** — individual pastries and cakes
* **Gallery (~5–6)** — abstract pastry compositions and organic forms; these can
  be the loosest and most artistic of the set

Start with the hero and one category icon, get the stroke weight and level of
detail right, then derive the rest from that established style.

Consistency across the set matters far more than any single illustration being
perfect.

If a given illustration is not turning out well, REMOVE it and let typography and
whitespace carry that section — an empty, well-composed space beats a weak
drawing. This is the guiding rule for the whole design: when in doubt, remove
rather than add.

## Responsive Design

The design must look excellent on:

* Desktop (1280px)
* Tablet (768px)
* Mobile (375px)

Design mobile intentionally rather than simply shrinking the desktop layout.
Tap targets must be at least 44×44px.

